fisheries organizations in Europe will  be closely following events in Brussels  during the next agricultural and  Fisheries Council on the sixteenth and  seventeenth of December every year on  the basis of a commission proposal  prepared upon scientific advice member  states have to decide how much fish and  of what kind can be caught the following  year in European waters and Beyond total  allowable catches and fishing quotas for  member states in 2014 will be fixed for  the stocks in the Atlantic the North Sea  the Baltic and the Black Sea as well as  for international fisheries in which EU  vessels participate the main objective  is to avoid overfishing and to achieve  maximum sustainable yields by 2015 the  belief is that managing stocks according  to maximum sustainable yield which is  the largest catch that can be taken from  a fish stock over an indefinite period  without harming it will in the end lead  to larger stocks and higher catch  potential and so far it's proving true  recent news published by the Commission  show an increase in stocks exploited at  maximum sustainable yield levels and a  general decrease in overfishing next to  the intense negotiations on fisheries  ministers will also listen to Commission  proposals to further promote European  agriculture in both the internal and  export markets faced with an  increasingly competitive global market  small farmers have a hard time when it  comes to selling their products abroad  the idea is that by explaining to  consumers the standards and the quality  what European agriculture puts on the  table EU promotional programs can help  European producers better place their  products at home but also abroad finally  the council will officially rubber stamp  the reform of the Common Agricultural  Policy kick-starting a new set of  measures to make it more competitive  sustainable and to breathe new look to  Europe's rural areas
 

